WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.070
Early this winter, the hundreds of climbers making plans for spring-summit attempts on Mount Qomolangma suddenly faced a new set of rules.
今冬之初，数百名正在筹划春季冲顶珠穆朗玛峰的登山者突然面临一套新规定。

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:20.570
In December, the Nepalese government decreed that it would no longer issue permits to blind, solo, or double-amputee mountaineers for any of its high peaks.
12月，尼泊尔政府颁布法令，规定今后不再向盲人登山者、单人登山者或双侧截肢的登山者发放该国任何一座高峰的登山许可证。

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:29.070
Furthermore, all expeditions would have to employ at least one Sherpa and would be forbidden from using helicopters to reach high camps.
此外，所有登山队都必须至少雇用一名夏尔巴人向导，并且不得乘直升机抵达高海拔营地。

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:47.350
The regulations fit a pattern established by Nepal’s Ministry of Tourism, which in the past few years has issued a series of proclamations—climbers must announce plans to set records, trekkers must carry location beacons—that suggest improved management of its high-altitude peaks.
这些规定符合尼泊尔旅游部确立的一贯做法：过去几年里，该部发布了一系列公告——登山者若打算创纪录，必须公布计划；徒步旅行者必须携带定位信标——这些公告让人觉得该国高海拔山峰的管理有所改善。

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:56.760
Each new declaration generates a rush of international news reports about authorities making strides toward addressing safety at the top of the world.
每一项新声明都会引发一波国际新闻报道，称当局在解决世界之巅的安全问题上取得了长足进展。

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.780
The truth is a lot more complicated.
真实情况要复杂得多。

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.020
Mountaineering is big business in Nepal.
登山在尼泊尔是一门大生意。

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:15.610
Industry experts estimate that it generates some $26.5 million in tourism income each year, with around $11 million of that coming from Qomolangma climbers alone.
据业内专家估计，登山每年带来约2650万美元的旅游收入，其中仅攀登珠峰的登山者就贡献了约1100万美元。

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:24.000
The enduring obsession of the Western media with tragic deaths on these far-off snowy peaks has resulted in a lot of free marketing.
西方媒体长久以来一直热衷于报道这些遥远雪峰上的悲惨死亡事件，由此带来了大量免费宣传。

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:39.510
Nepal’s Ministry of Tourism, perhaps concerned that all the morbid tales might drive climbers to Qomolangma’s less used Chinese side, has gained some control of that narrative by broadcasting more positive developments through the Nepalese press.
尼泊尔旅游部或许是担心种种骇人的死亡故事可能会把登山者赶到珠峰较少有人走的中国一侧，便通过尼泊尔新闻界播报更多积极进展，在一定程度上掌控了这种叙事。

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:51.080
But the rules announced to date would do nothing to mitigate the dangers of climbing Qomolangma even if Nepal had the resources and conviction to enforce them, which it doesn’t.
但迄今公布的这些规定，即便尼泊尔有资源、有决心执行，也丝毫无助于减轻攀登珠峰的危险，而尼泊尔并没有这样的资源和决心。

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.890
Making a huge, hugely popular mountain safer is possible.
让一座体量巨大、又极受欢迎的山峰变得更安全，是可以做到的。

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:04.530
On Alaska’s Denali, full-time climbing rangers conduct safety checks of many teams and are mobilized for rescue operations.
在阿拉斯加的德纳里峰，全职登山巡护员会对许多登山队实施安全检查，还会被调派执行救援行动。

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:14.520
On Argentina’s Aconcagua, rangers patrol all high camps, and until recently, permit fees included the cost of helicopter rescues.
在阿根廷的阿空加瓜峰，巡护员在所有高海拔营地巡逻，而且直到不久前，许可证费用中还包含直升机救援的开销。

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.700
Adopting similar policies in Nepal would be a good start.
在尼泊尔采取类似的政策，会是一个良好的开端。

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:41.930
A longer list of true reforms would include ordering all climbers to have previously summited a 7,000-meter peak, requiring non-guides working above Base Camp to take a course at the Khumbu Climbing Center (hundreds have done so since it was founded in 2003), and capping the total number of climbers on the mountain at 500 per season, including support staff.
若把真正的改革措施列得更全一些，还会包括：规定所有登山者此前必须登顶过一座7000米级的山峰；要求在大本营以上工作的非向导人员到昆布攀登中心参加培训课程（自该中心2003年成立以来，已有数百人参加过）；以及把每个登山季山上的登山者总数限制在500人，后勤保障人员也计算在内。

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.720
That last policy would both reduce dangerous crowding and help keep the mountain clean.
最后这项政策既能减少危险的拥堵，又有助于保持山上的清洁。

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:56.990
Unfortunately, these kinds of rules are less likely than ever to be instituted on Qomolangma, owing to the rise of budget guiding companies.
遗憾的是，由于低价向导公司的兴起，这类规定在珠峰付诸实行的可能性比以往任何时候都小。

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:10.840
Beginning in the early 1990s, Western outfitters established commercial mountaineering on the Nepal side of the peak by attracting clients willing to pay as much as $65,000 to be guided to the summit.
从20世纪90年代初开始，西方登山服务商招揽愿意花多达65000美元请向导带领自己登顶的客户，由此在珠峰的尼泊尔一侧开创了商业登山。

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:18.840
That business model dominated for more than two decades, bringing an estimated 9,000 paying climbers to Base Camp.
这种商业模式在二十多年里一直占据主导地位，据估计把9000名付费登山者带到了大本营。

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:25.850
Consequently, Qomolangma earned a reputation as a magnet for the rich, ambitious, and inexperienced.
因此，珠峰有了这样一个名声：它像磁石一样，吸引着有钱、有雄心又缺乏经验的人。

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.300
As in many markets, savvy entrepreneurs saw opportunities for disruption.
和许多市场的情况一样，精明的创业者看到了颠覆现有格局的机会。

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:44.560
Lower-cost guiding companies, some founded by Westerners and others by Nepalese, slowly gained traction by offering Qomolangma climbs for as little as a third of the going rate among high-end outfitters.
收费较低的向导公司有的由西方人创办，有的由尼泊尔人创办，它们提供珠峰攀登服务，价格低至高端登山服务商现行价格的三分之一，由此慢慢站稳了脚跟。

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:54.350
Then came 2014, when 16 Sherpas died after a serac collapsed onto the Khumbu Icefall, part of the main route from Base Camp to Camp I.
接着就到了2014年：一座冰塔崩塌，砸落在昆布冰瀑上，16名夏尔巴人遇难；昆布冰瀑是大本营通往一号营地这条主路线上的一段。

00:03:55.150 --> 00:04:09.050
In the wake of that tragedy, a small group of Sherpas demanded that the Nepalese government establish regulations that would improve working conditions, increase pay, boost life-insurance coverage, and provide a funeral stipend.
那场悲剧发生后，一小群夏尔巴人要求尼泊尔政府制定规章，以改善工作条件、增加薪酬、提高人寿保险的保障额度，并提供丧葬补助。

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:20.070
Ultimately, Sherpas received a bit more insurance—the minimum payout was doubled from $5,500 to $11,000—but not much else.
最终，夏尔巴人多得到了一点保险——最低赔付额从5500美元翻了一番，达到11000美元——但除此之外就没得到多少了。

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:29.740
Partly in response to media attention to these events, Nepali-owned guiding companies have continued to gain influence and market share on Qomolangma.
部分是因为媒体关注这些事件，尼泊尔人拥有的向导公司在珠峰的影响力和市场份额持续扩大。

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.820
The shift away from foreign control of the mountain is welcomed by many in the climbing community.
这座山正逐渐摆脱外国人的掌控，这一转变受到登山界许多人的欢迎。

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:48.120
Another positive development: lower-cost operators are increasing diversity on Qomolangma, attracting climbers from China’s and India’s burgeoning middle classes with aggressive pricing.
另一个积极的进展是：收费较低的运营商正在让珠峰上的登山人群更加多元，它们以低价攻势吸引来自中国和印度日益壮大的中产阶层的登山者。

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:58.560
According to numbers from the Himalayan Database, in 2010, four Indian and eight Chinese climbers attempted the mountain, just 6 percent of the total.
根据喜马拉雅数据库的数据，2010年有4名印度登山者和8名中国登山者尝试攀登珠峰，仅占总人数的6%。

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:06.550
Last year, Chinese and Indian clients accounted for 60 of the 199 Nepal-side summits.
去年，在尼泊尔一侧的199人次登顶中，中国和印度客户占了60人次。

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:20.190
Unfortunately, in the absence of substantive government oversight, some of the budget companies are making Qomolangma more dangerous by flooding the already overcrowded route with novice climbers led by inexperienced guides.
遗憾的是，由于缺乏政府的实质性监管，一些低价公司把大批新手登山者塞进本已过度拥挤的路线，带领他们的向导也缺乏经验，这让珠峰变得更加危险。

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:29.010
Any operators charging less for guided climbs are prone to bolster profits through scale, booking dozens of clients on expeditions.
凡是在向导攀登服务上收费较低的运营商，往往都会靠扩大规模来增加利润，在登山队里安排几十名客户。

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.870
(The most respected outfitters set a maximum of ten.)
（最受尊敬的登山服务商把上限定为十人。）

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:50.760
Putting aside 2014’s tragedy and 2015’s earthquake-induced avalanche, which killed at least 17 people at Base Camp, 12 of the 17 climbers who died on the South Col route between 2011 and 2017 appear to have been clients of budget outfitters.
撇开2014年的悲剧和2015年地震引发的雪崩（这场雪崩在大本营造成至少17人死亡）不谈，2011年至2017年间在南坳路线上遇难的17名登山者中，似乎有12人是低价登山服务商的客户。

00:05:51.560 --> 00:06:07.050
During last year’s peak season, Kathmandu-based Seven Summit Treks, known for bringing large groups of climbers to Qomolangma, allegedly promoted a young support staffer named Sange to guide Qomolangma and assigned him to an older Pakistani client.
去年登山旺季期间，总部位于加德满都、以带大批登山者前往珠峰而闻名的七峰探险公司，据称把一名叫桑格的年轻后勤人员提拔为珠峰向导，并派他带一名年纪较大的巴基斯坦客户。

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:12.120
The pair reached the summit late in the day and got into trouble on their descent.
两人当天很晚才登顶，下山途中遇险。

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:17.270
They had to be rescued by experienced Sherpas from another Nepalese outfitter.
他们只得靠另一家尼泊尔登山服务商经验丰富的夏尔巴人前来营救。

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.310
Sange later had all his fingers amputated due to severe frostbite.
桑格后来因严重冻伤，手指全部被截除。

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.220
Veteran guides are reacting to all this in different ways.
资深向导们正以各自不同的方式应对这一切。

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:36.540
Adrian Ballinger, founder of the California outfitter Alpenglow, has abandoned the Nepal side of Qomolangma and is instead leading teams from China.
加州登山服务商Alpenglow的创始人阿德里安·巴林杰已经放弃了珠峰的尼泊尔一侧，转而从中国一侧带队。

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:49.550
As he explained it, the higher risk from natural dangers (avalanches, seracs, crevasses), the low standards of other outfitters, and Nepal’s mismanagement add up to an unacceptable environment.
按他的解释，自然危险（雪崩、冰塔、冰裂缝）带来的更高风险、其他登山服务商的低标准以及尼泊尔的管理不善，加在一起，使那里的环境令人无法接受。

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:56.230
Several other prominent guides have come to the same conclusion, including Austrian Lukas Furtenbach.
其他几位知名向导也得出了同样的结论，其中包括奥地利人卢卡斯·富尔滕巴赫。

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.360
Others are staying put.
另一些人则留在原地。

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:17.760
International Mountain Guides co-owner Eric Simonson, whose first expedition on Qomolangma was in 1982, insists that upgrades in route-making through the Khumbu Icefall, and the establishment of dual ropes in areas prone to bottlenecks, have made the Nepal side safer, even as the crowds have grown.
国际登山向导公司的共同所有人埃里克·西蒙森1982年首次到珠峰探险，他坚称，穿越昆布冰瀑的修路工作有所改进，加上在容易出现瓶颈的路段设置了双绳，尽管登山人群越来越多，这些举措还是让尼泊尔一侧更加安全了。

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.160
Qomolangma remains the ultimate conquest for many climbers.
对许多登山者来说，珠峰仍然是终极的征服目标。

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:33.170
And while most embrace the risk of high-altitude mountaineering, few understand that the biggest dangers are all too often the result of economics, not the forces of nature.
而且，虽然大多数人都欣然接受高海拔登山的风险，但很少有人明白，最大的危险往往是经济因素造成的，而不是自然力量造成的。

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.920
Ultimately, the top priority of many tourism officials and outfitters isn’t safety.
说到底，许多旅游官员和登山服务商的头等大事并不是安全。

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:40.990
It’s the bottom line.
利润才是。
